  data:  # 561a
    kind: invariant  # 561a
    title: "Python stable_id formula too coarse: 91% collision rate on self-analysis (all @dataclass classes in same module collide)"  # 561a
    status: violated  # 561a
    priority: 2  # 561a
    parent:  # 561a
    tags:  # 561a
    - adr_0014  # 561a
    - self_analysis  # 561a
    - dogfooding  # 561a
    isbefore: []  # 561a
    duplicate_of: []  # 561a
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 561a
    pr_ref:  # 561a
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 4).**\n\n**Symptom.** On hypergumbo's own self-analysis:\n\n- 32,253 total nodes; 29,367 carry `stable_id`\n- Only 3,184 distinct stable_ids across those 29,367 nodes\n- **26,742 nodes (91% of stable_id'd nodes) are in collision groups** (size ≥ 2)\n- Top collision groups: one stable_id covers 7,902 nodes (mostly `__init__` methods), another covers 5,500 method nodes, another covers 4,006 class nodes\n\n**Specific cascade in `ir.py`:** 5 distinct Python `@dataclass`-decorated classes — `Symbol`, `Edge`, `Span`, `AnalysisRun`, `ExternalRef` — all share `stable_id=sha256:1e29284a8c92ef81`. Their `to_dict` methods (4 of them, one per class) cascade-collide on `sha256:babdcc9ce374313f` because containing-class identity is the only thing that could distinguish them and it's already lost upstream.\n\n**Root cause.** `packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/py.py:1526` `_compute_stable_id` hashes only:\n\n```python\nsig = f\"{kind}:{param_count}:{arity_flags}:{decorators_str}:{containing_stable_id}\"\n```\n\nFor two `@dataclass` classes in the same module, ALL five inputs are identical:\n- `kind = \"class\"` (same)\n- `param_count = 0` (same — classes have no params here)\n- `arity_flags = \"False,False,False\"` (same)\n- `decorators_str = \"dataclass\"` (same)\n- `containing_stable_id = <module's stable_id>` (same)\n\n→ identical hash → collision.\n\nFor methods inside those classes, `containing_stable_id` resolves to the class's stable_id — which itself collides — so the cascade carries.\n\n**Invariant.** `stable_id` must distinguish distinct symbols within the same compilation unit. The `Symbol.stable_id` docstring at `ir.py:204-359` promises \"Semantic identity hash (survives renames/moves)\". Collision rate of 91% means that for 91% of nodes, `stable_id` does not uniquely identify the symbol — only the symbol's category. The \"survives renames\" half of the promise is preserved (anchor symbols are stable across 4 baselines spanning 6 weeks), but the \"semantic identity\" half is broken.\n\n**Relationship to existing tracker items.**\n\n- `WI-jagus-…` (`done`) added `containing_module_stable_id` to the Python formula and bumped scheme to v2. The fix landed (formula includes `containing_stable_id`), but it does not address same-module same-decorator class collisions because the formula itself remains too coarse.\n- `INV-popop-…` (P1, **status `holding` — deprecated per AGENTS.md**) covers route-specific collisions (`stable_id=\"GET\"` for all GET routes). Different class of problem.\n- ADR-0014 documents the broader phased rollout. The class-level collision case is consistent with the documented \"Implementation gap\" section but no specific tracker item exists for it.\n\n**Fix proposal.**\n\nEither of the following would close the same-module class case:\n\n1. **Include class fields/methods in class signature.** Extend the hash inputs for `ClassDef` with `len(body)`, base-class names (already in `extends` edges), and field names. Smallest semantic change. Same-shape classes still collide (e.g., two `@dataclass` classes with identical field names + types in the same module). Probably acceptable — that's a genuine semantic-identity collision, not an artifact.\n\n2. **Include source span or content fingerprint as a tiebreaker.** Loses \"survives moves\" within the file but preserves \"survives renames\". Cheap to implement.\n\n3. **Distinguish stable_id from canonical_name path.** Where stable_id is purely structural identity, canonical_name (`mymodule.MyClass.method`) carries the disambiguating path. Consumers that want unique tracking should join on the pair `(stable_id, canonical_name)` rather than stable_id alone. Document this contract on the Symbol docstring; ADR-0014 should reflect it. This is a docs change with no code impact, but it's the most honest reflection of what the implementation actually delivers.\n\nRecommended sequencing: (3) immediately (docs); then (1) or (2) in the ADR-0014 Phase 4 sweep.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n- `packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/py.py:1526-1568` — `_compute_stable_id`\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/ir.py:204-359` — Symbol docstring promising \"semantic identity\"\n- `docs/adr/0014-generalized-symbol-identity.md` — phased rollout context\n- INV-popop (route-specific sibling)\n- WI-jagus (done; containing_module addition, insufficient for same-module classes)\n\n**Notebook reference.** `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/notebookjournal_05162026_0438.md` Round 4 section."  # 561a
    fields:  # 561a
      statement: "stable_id must distinguish distinct symbols within the same compilation unit. Symbol.stable_id docstring promises 'Semantic identity hash (survives renames/moves)'. Collision rate of 91% on a real codebase means stable_id identifies only the symbol's category, not the symbol itself, for the vast majority of nodes."  # 561a
      root_cause: "py.py:1526 _compute_stable_id hashes only 5 inputs: kind, param_count, arity_flags, decorators, containing_stable_id. Two @dataclass classes in the same module share all 5 inputs, so their stable_ids collide. Methods inside those classes cascade-collide because containing_stable_id resolves to the same colliding class hash. Confirmed by 4 distinct to_dict methods on Symbol/Edge/Span/AnalysisRun all sharing one stable_id. WI-jagus added containing_module to the formula but did not address the formula's intrinsic coarseness."  # 561a
